You are a senior full-stack engineer working inside this repository. Your job is to plan and implement a deployable MVP for “OnTime ERP – Attendance + Payroll Management”, replacing the existing attendance DB schema with a new one and expanding the UI and APIs accordingly.

NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES
- Do NOT ask me questions unless you truly cannot proceed.
- Inspect the repository first (stack, framework, ORM, DB, existing attendance module, UI routes/components).
- Follow existing conventions (folder structure, naming, auth patterns, migrations, DTOs, validators, tests).
- Minimal assumptions: if something is not explicitly specified, implement it in the simplest way that still supports the requirements (e.g., nullable fields, enums, config tables).
- Prefer incremental deliverables: make it run end-to-end with seed data before adding advanced features.

REQUIREMENTS (AUTHORITATIVE)
People & roles
- There are 3 roles: admin, employee (includes supervisor), worker. Admin has full access. Supervisor/employee can manage workers and take attendance, but cannot edit monetary fields (rates, salary components, payroll settings).
- Person ID is a random 6-character alphanumeric string (primary identifier used across the system).
- For each person store: identity number (national ID/passport), DOB, sex, phone, full name, face description/embeddings, plus attachments.
Documents
- Documents can be uploaded: admins upload for employees, employees upload for workers. Each document stores type, storage URL, uploaded timestamp.
Skills & pricing
- A worker has at least one skill. Each skill has a name. A skill is valued differently depending on location (price-per-skill-per-location).
Assignments
- A person can be assigned to multiple locations on specific shifts, with varying titles and rates (rate is monetary => admin-edit only).
Shifts
- Shift has start time and end time.
Attendance
- Supervisor can take attendance for themselves and for workers/employees for a shift at a location, storing check-in/out and the taken image for the day.
- Attendance is facial-recognition-based, and the system stores face data on Person and attendance image URLs. (Use existing face/image storage approach in repo.)
- Automation rules:
  - If no checkout is recorded by end-of-shift, system should automatically submit an overtime request (or mark as overtime pending review) OR mark absent depending on status and shift rules. Implement the simplest consistent rule: “missing checkout => overtime_pending if checked-in, else absent”, with a nightly job or on-demand reconciliation.
Salary components
- Admins configure salary components with: amount, kind (earning/deduction), type (overtime/incentive/allowance/base_salary), amount_type (fixed/per_day/per_hour/percentage).
Payroll UI
- Monthly attendance report dashboard: list workers with hours, rates, totals; search; show salaries, allowances, deductions, incentives; payroll group per location; etc.
- Supervisors handle overtime management for workers; admins can extract attendance reports, add locations, and manage all base data.

DELIVERABLES
1) A migration-based database schema that cleanly models: Person, Roles, Documents, Location, Shift, Assignment, Skill, SkillLocationPrice, Attendance, OvertimeRequest, SalaryComponent, PayrollPeriod, PayrollRun, PayrollRunEmployee, PayrollRunLine (minimal but complete).
2) Backend APIs with RBAC:
   - Admin: create supervisors/employees; manage locations/shifts/skills/pricing; manage salary components; set/modify rates/titles on assignments; run payroll; export attendance/payroll reports.
   - Supervisor/employee: create/edit workers (non-monetary fields only); take attendance; view attendance history; create/approve overtime for their workers (or submit for admin approval).
   - Worker: view own attendance and payslip (read-only).
3) UI updates to match the schema and features, expanding the existing attendance UI and adding payroll dashboard pages.
4) Replace old attendance schema and migrate safely (data migration optional; if too risky, provide a clear “reset DB” dev path and a prod migration strategy).

PHASED PLAN YOU MUST FOLLOW (OUTPUT FIRST, THEN IMPLEMENT)
Phase 0 — Repository discovery
- Identify stack (framework, ORM, DB), existing auth & roles, existing attendance tables, existing UI pages.
- List all impacted modules.
- Confirm how images/files are stored (local disk/S3/etc.) and reuse it.

Phase 1 — Schema design (write down ERD as tables + relations)
Implement tables with minimal fields required to satisfy requirements. Use:
- id: CHAR(6) for Person; other tables can use UUID or auto-increment per repo convention.
- created_at/updated_at timestamps per convention.

REQUIRED TABLES (MINIMAL ATTRIBUTES + RELATIONS)
A) users/auth (if repo already has users, map Person to existing user model; otherwise implement minimal)
- roles: id, name (admin, employee, worker)
- user_roles (or role_id on user), per existing pattern
- person: id CHAR(6) PK, role_id (or via user), full_name, identity_number, dob, sex, phone, status (active/suspended/terminated), face_embeddings/text, hire_date nullable
  - supervisors relationship: person.supervisor_id nullable FK -> person.id (simple reporting line)

B) documents
- document: id, person_id FK, type, storage_url, uploaded_at, uploaded_by_person_id FK (so we can enforce who uploaded)

C) locations/shifts/assignments
- location: id, name, city
- shift: id, starts_at (time), ends_at (time)
- assignment: id, person_id FK, location_id FK, shift_id FK, title, rate, effective_from nullable, effective_to nullable
  - enforce: only admin can set rate/title

D) skills/pricing
- skill: id, name
- person_skill: person_id FK, skill_id FK (worker must have >=1; enforce in app validation)
- skill_location_price: skill_id FK, location_id FK, price

E) attendance + overtime
- attendance: id, person_id FK (who attendance is for), taken_by_person_id FK (supervisor), location_id FK, shift_id FK, attendance_date (date), check_in (datetime nullable), check_out (datetime nullable), image_url nullable, status enum (present/absent/overtime_pending/inconsistent), notes nullable
  - uniqueness: (person_id, attendance_date, shift_id, location_id) unique
- overtime_request: id, person_id FK, attendance_id FK nullable, date, hours, status (pending/approved/rejected), created_by_person_id FK, approved_by_person_id FK nullable, created_at

F) payroll (minimal, but supports dashboard)
- payroll_period: id, month (YYYY-MM) or start/end dates, status (open/closed/locked)
- payroll_run: id, payroll_period_id FK, location_id FK nullable (allow per-location runs), status (draft/approved/locked), created_by_person_id, approved_by_person_id nullable, created_at
- payroll_run_employee: id, payroll_run_id FK, person_id FK, base_salary_amount (snapshot), gross, deductions, net, total_hours, total_days
- payroll_run_line: id, payroll_run_employee_id FK, component_id FK nullable, component_name_snapshot, kind (earning/deduction), amount, meta_json nullable

G) salary components
- salary_component: id, name, kind (earning/deduction), type (overtime/incentive/allowance/base_salary), amount_type (fixed/per_day/per_hour/percentage), amount (numeric), active_flag
- employee_component (optional but recommended for MVP payroll): id, person_id FK, component_id FK, value_override nullable, effective_from/to nullable

RELATIONSHIPS / CARDINALITIES TO ENFORCE IN CODE
- Person 1..N Documents
- Person N..N Skills via person_skill (but require >=1 for worker in validations)
- Skill N..N Location via skill_location_price
- Person 1..N Assignment; each Assignment belongs to exactly one shift and location
- Attendance belongs to one person; taken_by is a person (supervisor/employee); attendance references shift+location
- PayrollPeriod 1..N PayrollRun; PayrollRun 1..N PayrollRunEmployee; PayrollRunEmployee 1..N PayrollRunLine

Phase 2 — Implement migrations/models/entities + seed data
- Write migrations (and Prisma/TypeORM/Eloquent/etc. models) with indexes and FKs.
- Seed: one admin, one supervisor, a few workers, locations, shifts, skills, pricing, components.

Phase 3 — APIs + RBAC
Implement endpoints (names per repo convention):
ADMIN endpoints:
- POST/PUT/GET persons (create supervisor/employee/worker; admin can edit all)
- CRUD locations, shifts, skills, skill-location pricing
- CRUD salary components
- CRUD assignments (rate/title protected)
- attendance report export (CSV) and monthly attendance summary
- payroll: create period, run payroll, approve/lock, view dashboard and employee breakdown
SUPERVISOR endpoints:
- CRUD workers (restricted fields: cannot edit rate, salary components, pricing, payroll)
- take attendance (check-in/out with image_url)
- overtime: approve or submit
WORKER endpoints:
- view own attendance
- view own payroll results (if generated)

RBAC implementation:
- Use middleware/guards already in repo.
- Add field-level guards: supervisors cannot edit monetary fields (rate/title, salary components, pricing, payroll approvals).

Phase 4 — Attendance automation
- Implement reconciliation job/service:
  - For each attendance record with check_in and missing check_out after shift end + grace period => set status overtime_pending and create overtime_request with estimated hours (simple: now - shift_end or shift_end - check_in depending; keep minimal and deterministic).
  - If no check_in by shift end => mark absent (status=absent).
- Trigger: nightly cron, or on-demand endpoint restricted to admin, plus call it when generating reports/payroll.

Phase 5 — Payroll calculation (MVP logic)
For a given month/location:
- Compute per person:
  - total_hours from attendance (present + overtime-approved hours)
  - base earnings:
    - if base salary modeled as component or payroll_profile/basic salary: include it
  - allowances/incentives: sum employee_component or imported lines (MVP can be manual admin entry)
  - deductions: sum deduction components (MVP can be static)
  - overtime: if component exists, calculate from overtime approved hours * rate or component rule
- Store results in payroll_run_employee + payroll_run_line.
- Provide preview before approve; after approve, lock.

Phase 6 — UI expansion
- Reuse existing attendance UI. Update it to the new schema.
Admin UI:
- master data screens: locations, shifts, skills, pricing, salary components
- people management: create supervisors/workers, upload docs
- payroll dashboard: monthly attendance summary table (workers, hours, rate from assignment, totals), search/filter by location, click for details (components lines)
Supervisor UI:
- worker management (restricted)
- attendance taking flows (self + workers)
- overtime review/submit
Worker UI:
- my attendance list
- my payroll/payslip view (simple table)

Phase 7 — Replace old schema
- Identify old tables. Provide migration steps.
- If data migration is too risky, implement:
  - a script to drop old attendance tables and create new ones (dev),
  - and for prod: create new tables alongside and flip code paths, leaving old tables unused.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
- App builds and runs locally and on Hostinger environment.
- Admin can: add locations/shifts/skills/pricing/components, create supervisor & workers, assign workers, view attendance report, run payroll and view dashboard.
- Supervisor can: create/edit workers (restricted), take attendance with image URLs, submit/manage overtime.
- Worker can: view own attendance and payroll results.
- Attendance automation works (missing checkout => overtime_pending; missing check-in => absent) in a repeatable way.
- DB schema uses correct FKs and indexes; Person IDs are generated as 6-char alphanumeric and validated unique.

NOW DO THIS
1) Start with Phase 0: inspect repo, summarize stack and existing modules.
2) Produce the schema plan as SQL tables (or ORM schema) with relations and indexes.
3) Implement migrations and models.
4) Implement RBAC and the minimal endpoints.
5) Update UI routes/components to use the new APIs.
6) Add seed data and a short README on how to run the MVP.
